# =============================================================================
# 50-vpn-endpoint.nft — WireGuard handshake port (public internet)
# =============================================================================
# This is the ONLY rule that accepts traffic from the public internet.
#
# WireGuard needs its UDP listen port reachable from outside so that peers
# can initiate a handshake.  Everything else — all services — remains
# invisible and unreachable to non-mesh traffic.
#
# What happens on port 51820 UDP:
#   1. A peer sends an "Initiation" datagram (148 bytes, encrypted with
#      the server's public key and a fresh ephemeral key pair).
#   2. The server responds with a "Response" datagram (92 bytes).
#   3. Both sides derive shared session keys via the Noise_IKpsk2 handshake.
#   4. All subsequent data travels through the encrypted tunnel.
#      → Decrypted packets exit wg0 and enter the mesh rules in 20-mesh.nft.
#
# Stealth property:
#   Any UDP datagram to port 51820 that doesn't decrypt correctly is silently
#   discarded by WireGuard.  Port scanners receive no response and classify
#   the port as "open|filtered" — the server leaks no identifying information.
#
# BUG FIX (3A) — global vs per-source rate limit:
#   The original ruleset used a single shared token bucket:
#     limit rate 20/minute
#   This is a global counter.  A single attacker sending 20 packets/minute
#   exhausts the entire budget, blocking ALL other peers from completing
#   handshakes.  Effective denial-of-service against the WireGuard endpoint.
#
#   Fix: use a "meter" which creates an independent token bucket per source IP.
#   Now each peer has its own budget.  An attacker only blocks themselves.
#
# BUG FIX (v3-04) — inverted meter logic, IPv6 lockout, unbounded memory:
#   The v2 meter matched sources WITHIN the limit and accepted them; anything
#   else fell to an unconditional drop.  Three failure modes:
#     1. IPv6 handshakes: the meter keyed on "ip saddr" (IPv4-only match), so
#        every IPv6 handshake missed the accept and hit the drop — WireGuard
#        over IPv6 was silently impossible.
#     2. No "size" cap: meter memory was unbounded (kernel default 65535
#        anyway, but implicit limits are how you get surprised at 3am).
#     3. Fail-CLOSED: when the meter table filled, NEW sources could not be
#        tracked, never matched the accept, and were dropped — an attacker
#        who fills the meter locks every new peer out of the VPN.
#   v3 inverts the logic: the meter matches sources EXCEEDING the limit and
#   drops them; everything else falls through to a plain accept.  A full
#   meter now degrades to "no rate limiting" (fail-OPEN) — acceptable here
#   because WireGuard itself authenticates cryptographically and silently
#   discards anything that doesn't decrypt.  The rate limit is noise
#   reduction, not the security boundary; it must never become the outage.
#
# Rate-limit rationale:
#   WireGuard rekeys every ~3 minutes by default (REKEY_AFTER_TIME = 180s).
#   10 handshake datagrams/minute per source is generous for legitimate peers
#   and still leaves headroom for reconnects after roaming or network changes.
#   (v2 used 5/minute, which flirted with dropping legitimate retries —
#   handshake initiations retransmit every ~5s until answered.)
#
# Change the port if your WireGuard server uses a non-default listen port.
# Must match ListenPort in /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf.
# =============================================================================

table inet filter {
    chain input {

        # Per-source over-limit drop (IPv4).  Sources exceeding the budget
        # jump to a rate-limited log + drop; everyone else falls through.
        udp dport 51820 \
            meter wg_handshake_rl size 65535 { ip saddr timeout 60s limit rate over 10/minute } \
            jump wg_ratelimit_drop comment "vpn-endpoint: WG handshake per-source limit (IPv4)"

        # Per-source over-limit drop (IPv6), keyed per /64 — a single attacker
        # typically controls an entire /64, so per-address metering would let
        # them rotate through 2^64 fresh buckets (BUG v3-04.1).
        udp dport 51820 \
            meter wg_handshake_rl6 size 65535 { ip6 saddr and ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:: timeout 60s limit rate over 10/minute } \
            jump wg_ratelimit_drop comment "vpn-endpoint: WG handshake per-/64 limit (IPv6)"

        # The ONLY public accept in the entire ruleset.
        udp dport 51820 \
            counter accept comment "vpn-endpoint: WireGuard handshake (public, v4+v6)"
    }

    # Rate-limited log + unconditional drop for handshake-flood offenders.
    # Seeing this prefix repeatedly from one source indicates a scan or
    # handshake flood — worth investigating.
    chain wg_ratelimit_drop {
        limit rate 10/second burst 20 packets \
            log prefix "XNFT-WG-RATELIMIT: " level info \
            comment "vpn-endpoint: log handshake flood (rate-limited)"
        counter drop comment "vpn-endpoint: handshake rate-limit exceeded, drop"
    }
}
